messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C.\n####\n# Context:\nThe bald eagle eats the cow. The bald eagle is blue. The cat visits the bald eagle. The cat visits the rabbit. The cow is blue. The cow sees the rabbit. The rabbit eats the bald eagle. The rabbit is cold. The rabbit sees the cow. The rabbit visits the cat. If something visits the cow and it sees the cow then it visits the bald eagle. If something is nice then it is big. If something visits the bald eagle then it is nice. If something is cold and it visits the cow then the cow sees the cat. If something is round and it eats the bald eagle then it sees the bald eagle. If something visits the cat then it visits the rabbit. If something visits the rabbit and it sees the cow then it visits the cow.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or unknown? The rabbit is big.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Unknown', '<think>:.\nOkay, let\'s see. I need to figure out if the statement "The rabbit is big" is true, false, or unknown based on the given information. Alright, let\'s start by going through each of the premises step by step and see how they connect.\n\nFirst, let me list out all the given statements to have a clear overview:.\n\n1. The bald eagle eats the cow.\n2. The bald eagle is blue.\n3. The cat visits the bald eagle.\n4. The cat visits the rabbit.\n5. The cow is blue.\n6. The cow sees the rabbit.\n7. The rabbit eats the bald eagle.\n8. The rabbit is cold.\n9. The rabbit sees the cow.\n10. The rabbit visits the cat.\n11. If something visits the cow and it sees the cow, then it visits the bald eagle.\n12. If something is nice, then it is big.\n13. If something visits the bald eagle, then it is nice.\n14. If something is cold and it visits the cow, then the cow sees the cat.\n15. If something is round and it eats the bald eagle, then it sees the bald eagle.\n16. If something visits the cat, then it visits the rabbit.\n17. If something visits the rabbit and it sees the cow, then it visits the cow.\n\nThe question is whether the rabbit is big. So, I need to see if there\'s a chain of reasoning that leads to the rabbit having the property "big".\n\nFirst, let\'s note the properties each entity has so far:.\n\n- Bald eagle: eats the cow, is blue, is eaten by the rabbit.\n- Cat: visits the bald eagle and the rabbit.\n- Cow: is blue, is eaten by the bald eagle, is seen by the rabbit, and the cow sees the rabbit.\n- Rabbit: is cold, eats the bald eagle, sees the cow, visits the cat, and is visited by the cat (since the cat visits the rabbit, per statement 4).\n\nNow, let\'s look at the implications (statements 11 to 17). The key ones here are related to the rabbit\'s properties and whether they can lead to it being "big".\n\nStarting with the rabbit\'s properties: it\'s cold (statement 8) and it eats the bald eagle (statement 7). Also, the rabbit visits the cat (statement 10), which means according to statement 16 (if something visits the cat, then it visits the rabbit), but wait, that seems a bit circular. Wait, statement 16 is: If something visits the cat, then it visits the rabbit. So the rabbit visits the cat (statement 10), so applying statement 16 to the rabbit: since the rabbit visits the cat, then the rabbit must visit the rabbit? That\'s a bit confusing. Wait, maybe I need to parse that correctly.\n\nWait, statement 16: "If something visits the cat, then it visits the rabbit." So the antecedent is "something visits the cat", and the consequent is "it visits the rabbit". So if an entity X visits the cat, then X visits the rabbit. Since the rabbit itself visits the cat (statement 10), then substituting X with the rabbit, that would mean the rabbit must visit the rabbit. But visiting oneself might not be considered here, but maybe the rule still applies. However, this might not lead us anywhere useful. Let\'s hold that thought.\n\nLooking at the rabbit\'s properties: it\'s cold (statement 8). Let\'s check statement 14: "If something is cold and it visits the cow, then the cow sees the cat." The rabbit is cold (so first part is true), but does it visit the cow? Looking at the given statements, the rabbit\'s actions are visiting the cat (statement 10) and being visited by the cat (statement 4). The rabbit\'s visits are only to the cat. So the rabbit doesn\'t visit the cow. Therefore, the antecedent of statement 14 (cold and visits the cow) is false, so the implication is true but doesn\'t give us new info.\n\nNext, statement 17: "If something visits the rabbit and it sees the cow, then it visits the cow." Let\'s see. The rabbit visits the cat (statement 10), and the rabbit sees the cow (statement 9). Wait, the rabbit sees the cow (statement 9). So, if we consider the rabbit as the subject here, does the rabbit visit the rabbit (which would be the rabbit visiting itself)? Wait, that might not make sense. Alternatively, maybe there\'s another entity involved.\n\nAlternatively, let\'s see if the cat is involved here. The cat visits the rabbit (statement 4). Does the cat see the cow? The cow sees the rabbit (statement 6), but does the cat see the cow? Not mentioned directly. Hmm.\n\nLooking at statement 13: If something visits the bald eagle, then it is nice. The cat visits the bald eagle (statement 3), so the cat is nice (from 13). Then, from statement 12: if something is nice, then it is big. Therefore, the cat is big. But the question is about the rabbit being big. So that\'s a side note, but not directly helpful yet.\n\nNow, focusing on the rabbit. The rabbit eats the bald eagle (statement 7). Looking at statement 15: "If something is round and it eats the bald eagle, then it sees the bald eagle." The rabbit eats the bald eagle, but we don\'t know if the rabbit is round. Since the premise about roundness isn\'t given, the antecedent (round and eats bald eagle) might not hold, so that doesn\'t